is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router0, router6
diagnosis_text:
The reported router0→router1 Serial link failure is a misdiagnosis: both eth2 interfaces are physically UP, carrier is present, and router0 can ping router1's 10.16.1.2 with 0% loss. Two distinct configuration faults are responsible for the observed reachability failures.

**Fault 1 — router0, missing RIP daemon:** Router0's FRR instance runs only `zebra`, `mgmtd`, and `staticd` — there is no `ripd` process. The `show running-config` output confirms a completely empty routing-protocol section (no `router rip` block, no network statements). Consequently, router0 never participates in RIP exchange with router1. It holds no route to 192.168.200.0/24 (Location C) or other RIP-learned prefixes; pc4 receives an immediate "Destination Net Unreachable" from 172.16.10.1 (router0 itself) when attempting to reach pc13. This is the direct cause of Location A's isolation from Location C and most of the backbone. Fix: add a `router rip` block with the appropriate `network` statements (covering the 10.16.1.0/24 WAN link and 172.16.10.0/24 / 172.16.20.0/24 access subnets) to router0's FRR configuration and enable the `ripd` daemon.

**Fault 2 — router6, blanket ACL DROP on eth2 FORWARD chain:** An iptables (and mirrored nftables) policy on router6 drops all TCP, UDP, and ICMP arriving on eth2 (the 10.50.1.x uplink toward router3), with a single narrow exception allowing HTTP from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1. The counter already shows 30 dropped packets (30 × 84 B = 2520 B, matching our ICMP test probes). Any transit traffic from the router3/router4 side to 172.18.51.x is silently dropped, causing the bidirectional 192.168.200.x ↔ 172.18.51.x failure and also blocking pc4→172.18.51.x even when router0 holds a static route. Fix: remove or correct the DROP rules in the FORWARD chain on router6 to permit legitimate inter-site traffic.
=== END ===